WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:12.280
The ending may be the most critical part of
a film.

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:16.279
It’s easy to forget about a few weak spots
in a movie that ends well, but a bad ending

00:00:16.279 --> 00:00:23.029
can ruin an otherwise great movie.

00:00:23.029 --> 00:00:27.130
Crafting a good ending is hard, so it’s
rare when I see a film that doesn’t just

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:32.830
wrap things up in a satisfying way but manages
to make it the high point of the film.

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:39.050
This is why my favorite thing about writer/director
Damien Chazelle is how he crafts an ending.

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:43.050
My favorite part of both Whiplash and La La
Land are their endings.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:47.040
By exploring what these endings have in common,
I think we’ll learn a lot about what makes

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:50.370
them work so well.

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.900
With regards to plot both films have a pretty
simple ending.

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:58.290
There aren’t any complex plot points to
wrap up, there are no subplots hanging loose.

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:02.680
Because of this they’re both able to climax
with sequence that packages all the resolution

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:05.379
of the film into one scene.

00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:09.220
In both films there’s a feeling that everything
in the film has been building towards this

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:12.250
MOMENT, this specific SCENE.

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:17.500
By the final scene in both films, the narrative
force can be summed up by a single dramatic

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.720
question waiting to be answered.

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:24.670
Not every movie can have that sort of simplicity,
and not every movie should, but because both

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:30.650
of these films do, it maximizes the emotional
punch of both endings.

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:33.550
The two sequences are almost exactly the same
length.

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:36.950
Both over 15 minutes, these are long sequences.

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:41.670
From the beginning of each, you’re waiting
for a finale and Chazelle keeps you waiting

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:51.420
for a quarter of an hour.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:02:00.900
In Whiplash the emotional conflict is developed
between Fletcher 

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:06.259
and Andrew.

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:16.200
We’re waiting the entire film to see Andrew
show his full ability and receive recognition

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:17.730
for how good he is.

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:22.150
Chazelle withholds this from the audience
for the entire film, and just when we think

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:52.340
we’re about to see it, we see Andrew put
down once again.

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:54.340
The same thing happens in La La Land.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:03:00.870
The emotional conflict of the story exists
between Sebastian and Mia.

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:06.799
We are waiting for the moment when they end
up together happily ever after.

00:03:06.799 --> 00:03:16.340
But just before the end, that idea is shattered.

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:22.090
By placing a negative false climax, in the
lead up to the end, Chazelle puts us on edge

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:23.499
in both films.

00:03:23.499 --> 00:03:34.529
We realize the happy ending we hoped was coming
may not be.

00:03:34.529 --> 00:03:38.879
The simplicity, length, and false climax all
work together to create tension.

00:03:38.879 --> 00:03:44.400
And because of this tension simple things
begin to take on significant meaning.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:50.049
The smallest emotion on a face, becomes magnified
by the emotional context.

00:03:50.049 --> 00:03:54.739
These simple character interactions will be
enough to communicate what we need to know.

00:03:54.739 --> 00:03:58.029
Neither film relies on any kind of exposition
for it’s ending.

00:03:58.029 --> 00:04:17.250
We’re able to see, and most importantly
feel the climax of emotion.

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:21.830
Another reason these scenes are so powerful
is that they use music to communicate some

00:04:21.830 --> 00:04:41.130
of the emotion 
and tension.

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:45.470
Both the scene in Whiplash and La La land
are essentially music videos, but with the

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:56.530
context of 90 minutes of movie settings us
up for the payoff.

00:04:56.530 --> 00:05:25.340
Then Chazelle does something really brilliant,
he hits us with a second false climax, he

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.730
lets us see the happy ending.

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:39.300
We can enjoy the moment, the music, and see
the characters get what they wanted.

00:05:39.300 --> 00:06:09.830
But it doesn’t last.

00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:14.750
Chazelle then answers the dramatic questions
by mixing these two climaxes together.

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:18.840
We got to see the happy ending, but not in
the way we were expecting too.

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:24.310
Chazelle drives home the reality of the situation,
reminding us of what it cost for the characters

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:42.560
to get to this place.

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:49.090
The emotion of the disappointment, and the
joy of the happy ending combine, creating

00:06:49.090 --> 00:07:07.020
in the viewer the same conflicted emotions
the characters are facing in both endings.

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:12.530
We only have the option of feeling the answer
to the dramatic question posed, because it’s

00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:14.930
never spoken.

00:07:14.930 --> 00:07:22.630
When the payoff of each scene arrives, it
comes as a simple exchange.

00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:25.280
Two close ups.

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:26.280
Non-verbal communication.

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:29.990
We only have the option of feeling the answer
to the dramatic question posed, because it’s

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:30.990
not spoken.

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:46.280
The power of the music allows Chazelle to
use silence for emphasis in these moments.

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:50.500
The final similarity is that both movies end
directly after the emotional climax.

00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:53.960
There is no afterword, no time to come down
from the experience.

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:58.290
The credits just roll, and you’re just left
with the conclusion ringing in your ears.

00:07:58.290 --> 00:08:05.590
This helps that climactic moment stick in
your mind long after the film has ended.

00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:09.830
Thank you for watching, if you enjoy my channel
and content and would like to help support

00:08:09.830 --> 00:08:14.370
what I’m doing, check out my patreon, where
